\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{OpenGLCamera()} is upstream's camera mobject: a frame of five points
(center, left, right, bottom, top), a model matrix that places it at
\verb{(0, 0, 11)}, Euler angles (\code{theta}, \code{phi}, \code{gamma}) and the projection
and view matrices.
\item \code{Object3D(...)} is a node of a tree of objects with a model matrix,
a normal matrix and updaters; \code{Mesh()} adds vertex attributes, indices
and a shader, and \code{FullScreenQuad()} is a mesh covering the frame.
A scene takes meshes with \code{add()}, and \code{OpenGLRenderer} draws them
after the mobjects: a mesh whose shader is one of upstream's mesh
shaders (the \code{default}, \code{manim_coords}, \code{vertex_colors} and \code{test}
folders) is rasterized by their rules, and other GLSL code (a
\code{FullScreenQuad}'s too) runs in manimR's GLSL interpreter: the vertex
shader on the vertices and the fragment shader on the fragments, with
perspective-correct varyings, depth test and alpha blending. The
interpreter takes the scalar, vector and square matrix types, in, out,
uniform and const globals, user functions, \code{if}, \code{for}, \code{while},
\code{break}, \code{continue}, \code{return}, \code{discard}, structs and the common
built-in functions, and runs a geometry shader (points or triangles in,
a triangle strip out) between the two stages. A \code{sampler2D} uniform
holds a texture unit: \code{context$texture(size, components, data)} makes
a texture (bytes, the row at v = 0 first) and its \code{use(location)} binds
it, sampled linearly and repeated as moderngl does. Other samplers and
arrays inside structs raise an error, and
\code{Mesh$render()} of a mesh it cannot run needs an OpenGL context and
stops.
\item \code{Shader(context, name, source)} reads the GLSL code of a shader folder
under \code{SHADER_FOLDER} (or takes \code{source}, a list of
\code{vertex_shader}, \code{fragment_shader} and \code{geometry_shader} code) and keeps
it in \code{shader_program_cache} by name; \code{set_uniform()} stores a value for
a uniform the code declares and ignores others, as upstream.
\item \code{filter_attributes()} keeps the named attributes of shader data.
\item \code{build_matrix_lists()} groups a mobject's family by hierarchical model
matrix, and \code{triangulate_mobject()} gives the fill triangles of a
VMobject with upstream's texture coordinates and modes.
\item \code{affects_shader_info_id(func)} and \code{triggers_refreshed_triangulation(func)}
are upstream's decorators as functions: they wrap a method \code{func(self, ...)}.
\item \code{OpenGLRenderer()} renders scenes as upstream's renderer: it clears its
framebuffer to the background color, sets the perspective uniforms from
the camera (\code{refresh_perspective_uniforms()}) and draws each mobject.
The shader wrappers run upstream's GLSL rules in software, in the
order of upstream's \code{get_shader_wrapper_list()}: a VMobject's family
runs the quadratic_bezier_fill and quadratic_bezier_stroke shaders
(anti aliased fills, and strokes with upstream's joints and ends), an
\code{OpenGLSurface} the surface shader, an \code{OpenGLTexturedSurface} or
\code{OpenGLImageMobject} the textured_surface shader (nearest texel), and an
OpenGL point cloud the
true_dot shader. Points are placed as the shaders place them (rotated
by \code{camera_rotation}, scaled by \code{focal_distance / (focal_distance - z)}, fixed in frame mobjects in the default frame); triangles are
rasterized at pixel centers, lit per pixel by upstream's \code{add_light()},
and with \code{depth_test} (on by default for surfaces) tested against and
written to the framebuffer's depth buffer. A plain \code{ImageMobject}, on
which upstream's OpenGL renderer raises, is drawn by a grid camera.
\code{config$renderer = "opengl"} makes
scenes use it. \code{get_frame()} gives a \code{nativeRaster};
\code{get_raw_frame_buffer_object_data()} the pixels bottom row first, as
OpenGL reads them.
\item \code{render_opengl_vectorized_mobject_fill()},
\code{render_opengl_vectorized_mobject_stroke()} and their \verb{_with_matrix}
helpers build upstream's vertex buffers and uniforms and draw them as
upstream's projection shaders do (with \code{config$use_projection_fill_shaders}
and \code{config$use_projection_stroke_shaders}): the fill keeps the part of
each triangle on the side of its curve given by its texture mode, and the
stroke the points within \code{0.004 * width} of its curve.
\item \code{OpenGLSoftwareContext()} stands for moderngl's context: \code{framebuffer()},
\code{buffer()} and \code{simple_vertex_array()}, whose \code{render()} draws the
\code{vectorized_mobject_fill} and \code{vectorized_mobject_stroke} shaders.
\item \code{Window(renderer)} is the window of a live preview: an R graphics
device (an interactive session is needed) that shows the renderer's frame
on \code{swap_buffers()}; \code{handle_event()} waits for a mouse or key event
in it and passes it to the scene's handlers, on devices that report
events (X11 and Windows). Its size follows \code{config$window_size} and its position
\code{config$window_position} on the monitor, which is
\code{getOption("manimR.monitor")} (a list of \code{x}, \code{y}, \code{width} and \code{height})
or 1920 by 1080 pixels at the origin.
}
